INT. MOBILE MRI TRAILER - PARKED BEHIND RURAL CLINIC - MORNING

A white box on wheels with a humming magnet at its heart. A glass partition. On the other side, a patient lies on a sliding table with his head in a cage.

DR. SABINA ROTHENBERG, forties, radiologist, sits at the console, tapping the mouse. Beside her, TECHNOLOGIST FELIX AGYEMAN, thirties, adjusts a dial. On the table, ARMANDO KESSLER, sixty, a farmer, looks at the ceiling.

SABINA
(into the mic)
Mr. Kessler, this is the last sequence. Just breathe normally.

ARMANDO (V.O.)
(through speaker)
Doc, you've said that three times.

SABINA
And I mean it more each time.

Felix smiles briefly. The magnet knocks in a steady rhythm.

FELIX
Motion's good. He's a model patient.

SABINA
He's lying very still.

FELIX
That's what I said.

SABINA
Felix, when did he last eat?

FELIX
Six, he said. He drove forty miles.

SABINA
Mm.

The images build on the monitor, slice by slice. Sabina leans closer. Her smile slackens.

FELIX
Everything okay?

SABINA
Yes.

FELIX
You went quiet.

SABINA
I'm reading.

The magnet knocks. Felix watches her for a long moment, and then looks at the screen.

FELIX
Is that --

SABINA
Don't.

FELIX
It's bilateral.

SABINA
I said don't.

FELIX
Sabina.

SABINA
Not until he's out of the machine.

Felix slowly takes his hand off the dial.

ARMANDO (V.O.)
(through speaker)
Everything all right in there? You two have gone awful quiet.

Sabina presses the button.

SABINA
Everything's fine, Mr. Kessler. A few more minutes.

She releases it and sits very still, and Felix, without a word, reaches over and turns the monitor away from the glass.

The magnet knocks. And knocks.
